\section*{An exponential threshold-repetition bound for arbitrary finite entangled games}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Entangled-game repetitions are claimed to have exponentially small probability of winning above the single-game value by any fixed gap, for arbitrary question correlations and finite-dimensional joint strategies.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} Consider a finite game \(G\) with two players, one simultaneous answer round and a Boolean acceptance rule. Its entangled value is \(v=\omega^*(G)<1\), the supremum over finite-dimensional strategies. Put \(d=|\mathcal A||\mathcal B|\), the product of answer-set sizes. For \(k\) independently sampled question pairs and any joint finite-dimensional strategy, let \(W_k\) count accepted coordinates. The claimed universal \(\kappa_0>0\) gives
\[
 \mathbb P[W_k\ge\lceil(v+\delta)k\rceil]
 \le\exp\!\left[-\frac{\kappa_0\delta^5}{1+\log d}k\right]
 \qquad(0<\delta<1-v,\ k\ge1).
\]
The same estimate holds after taking the supremum over all finite-dimensional joint strategies.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Conditioning on a tested subset is represented by one common purification of effects. Information and error amplification bounds convert excessive threshold success into a better single-game strategy; universal estimates give the fifth-power gap rate and fixed question laws permit a cubic refinement.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Entangled-game repetitions are claimed to have exponentially small probability of winning above the single-game value by any fixed gap, for arbitrary question correlations and finite-dimensional joint strategies. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 277, the documented exponential repetition rate has a $\delta^{13}$ factor, rather than the manuscript improved $\delta^5$ rate; the distribution-dependent cubic refinement is also not selected. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{EntangledGames.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.ThresholdParallelRepetition.threshold\_parallel\_repetition}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

